<h1>State machines</h1>
<p>Characters and props play animations through a state machine: a small graph of named states (idle, run, attack) with rules for moving between them. The <code>demi.animation</code> module drives that graph from scripts.</p>

<h2>What a state machine is</h2>
<p>A state machine is a set of <strong>states</strong>, each pointing at a clip to play, plus <strong>transitions</strong> that say when to switch. You do not scrub frames by hand: you set parameters (speed, a trigger), and the machine picks the state. Layer and blend-space data can be authored, with the current presentation limits described below.</p>

<h2>States</h2>
<p>A state names a clip and can carry timed markers. In a 3D model the clip is <code>model_clip_name</code>; in a 2D sprite sheet it is <code>sprite_clip</code>.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "states": {
    "run": {
      "model_clip_name": "Run_Loop",
      "duration": 0.8,
      "events": [
        { "time": 0.2, "name": "footstep_left" },
        { "time": 0.7, "name": "footstep_right" }
      ]
    },
    "idle": { "model_clip_name": "Idle_Loop", "duration": 1.0 },
    "skyforge_slam": { "model_clip_name": "Slam", "duration": 0.6, "loop": false }
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>The key in <code>states</code> is the stable state name used by transitions and Lua.</li>
  <li><code>model_clip_name</code> / <code>sprite_clip</code> — which clip this state plays (one or the other, depending on the entity).</li>
  <li><code>events</code> — array of <code>{time, name}</code> markers. <code>time</code> is seconds into the authored state duration; <code>name</code> is emitted in an <code>animation_event</code> payload (see <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'events-and-timers'}) }}">Events &amp; timers</a>). Give timed states a positive <code>duration</code> to make loop and <code>finished</code> timing meaningful.</li>
</ul>
<p>Markers fire whether or not anyone listens. Subscribe to <code>animation_event</code> and inspect its <code>entity_id</code>, <code>state</code>, and <code>name</code> fields; the marker name is payload data, not the event channel.</p>

<h2>Blend spaces</h2>
<p>A blend space samples named states by one or two numeric parameters. Its key must match the active state name; the example samples <code>idle</code> and <code>run</code> while <code>locomotion</code> is active. The machine records sample weights, but current playback chooses the highest-weight state; it does not interpolate their visual poses.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "states": {
    "locomotion": { "model_clip_name": "Idle_Loop", "duration": 1.0 },
    "idle": { "model_clip_name": "Idle_Loop", "duration": 1.0 },
    "run": { "model_clip_name": "Run_Loop", "duration": 0.8 }
  },
  "initial_state": "locomotion",
  "parameters": { "speed": 0 },
  "blend_spaces": {
    "locomotion": {
      "parameter_x": "speed",
      "points": [
        { "x": 0, "state": "idle" },
        { "x": 4, "state": "run" }
      ]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>The key in <code>blend_spaces</code> names the state on which the space is sampled.</li>
  <li><code>parameter_x</code> — the parameter name sampled to pick the mix.</li>
  <li><code>points</code> — <code>{x, state}</code> samples referencing declared states. The current parameter value determines the neighboring sample weights; the largest weight wins for playback.</li>
</ul>
<p>Set the parameter with <code>set_number</code> (below). The chosen clip can change as the largest sample weight changes.</p>

<h2>Layers</h2>
<p>Layer data describes a secondary state, weight, additive mode, and bone mask. The runtime stores these on the animation player, and <code>set_layer_weight</code> changes the stored weight. The current 3D renderer samples one base clip and does not combine layer poses, so this does not yet produce a walk-and-cast upper-body animation.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "layers": {
    "upper": {
      "state": "cast",
      "weight": 0.8,
      "additive": false,
      "mask": ["spine", "chest", "arm_l", "arm_r"]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>The key in <code>layers</code> is the layer name used by <code>set_layer_weight</code>.</li>
  <li><code>state</code> — which state plays on this layer.</li>
  <li><code>weight</code> — how much the layer contributes (0…1).</li>
  <li><code>additive</code> — authored intent for additive layering; visual pose composition is not yet implemented.</li>
  <li><code>mask</code> — authored bone names for a layer; the current renderer does not apply the mask.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Transitions</h2>
<p>A transition moves from one state to another when its condition holds. <code>blend_duration</code> records transition progress for Lua and preview state, but the current renderer switches the played clip without a visual cross-fade.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "transitions": {
    "start_run": { "from": "idle", "to": "run", "condition": "greater",
                   "parameter": "speed", "threshold": 0.1, "blend_duration": 0.15 },
    "stop_run": { "from": "run", "to": "idle", "condition": "less",
                  "parameter": "speed", "threshold": 0.1, "blend_duration": 0.2 },
    "start_slam": { "from": "*", "to": "skyforge_slam", "condition": "trigger",
                    "parameter": "slam" },
    "end_slam": { "from": "skyforge_slam", "to": "idle",
                  "condition": "finished", "blend_duration": 0.05 }
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>from</code> / <code>to</code> — state names.</li>
  <li><code>condition</code> — <code>always</code>, <code>finished</code>, <code>trigger</code>, <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>greater</code>, or <code>less</code>. The latter five use <code>parameter</code>; <code>greater</code> and <code>less</code> also use <code>threshold</code>. <code>finished</code> checks the state's positive authored duration.</li>
  <li><code>blend_duration</code> — seconds of tracked transition progress; visual pose cross-fading is not yet implemented.</li>
</ul>
<p>Without <code>finished</code>, a one-shot attack never falls back to idle on its own. Pair one-shot states with a <code>finished</code> transition out. The target and source states must both be declared in the same machine.</p>

<h2>Driving the machine from Lua</h2>
<pre><code>local Animation = require("demi.animation")

Animation.set_number(self.id, "speed", 2.5)       -- numeric parameter
Animation.set_bool(self.id, "grounded", true)     -- boolean parameter
Animation.trigger(self.id, "jump")                -- one-shot trigger

Animation.play(self.id, "skyforge_slam")          -- force a state
print(Animation.state(self.id))                   -- current state name, or ""

Animation.set_speed(self.id, 1.25)                -- playback rate multiplier
local t = Animation.normalized_time(self.id)      -- 0…1 position in the current clip</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Takes</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.set_number(id, name, value)</code></td><td>Entity, parameter name, number</td><td><code>true</code> when applied</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.set_bool(id, name, value)</code></td><td>Entity, parameter name, boolean</td><td><code>true</code> when applied</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.trigger(id, name)</code></td><td>Entity, trigger name</td><td><code>true</code> when armed — a one-shot condition until consumed</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.play(id, state)</code></td><td>Entity, state name</td><td><code>true</code> when entered immediately</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.state(id)</code></td><td>Entity</td><td>Current state name string; <code>""</code> when unavailable</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.set_speed(id, rate)</code></td><td>Entity, playback multiplier</td><td><code>true</code> when applied</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.normalized_time(id)</code></td><td>Entity</td><td>Number <code>0</code>…<code>1</code>: position within the current clip</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.transition(id)</code></td><td>Entity</td><td>Transition info table: <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>progress</code> (0…1), <code>active</code> boolean</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.set_layer_weight(id, layer, weight)</code></td><td>Entity, layer name, 0…1</td><td><code>true</code> when applied</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Animation.set_root_motion(id, on)</code></td><td>Entity, boolean</td><td><code>true</code> when applied — uses the current state's authored <code>root_motion_track</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><code>set_number</code> and <code>set_bool</code> create or update a parameter on an existing machine; <code>trigger</code> arms a named trigger even if no transition uses it. Both return <code>false</code> when the entity has no machine. <code>Animation.state</code> returns an empty string for an entity with no machine.</p>
<pre><code>local Animation = require("demi.animation")
local Input = require("demi.input")

function SkyforgeSlam:on_update(dt)
    self.speed = self.speed or 0
    Animation.set_number(self.id, "speed", self.speed)
    if Input.pressed("attack") then
        Animation.trigger(self.id, "slam") -- needs a transition with condition "trigger", parameter "slam"
    end
end</code></pre>
<p><code>set_number</code> feeds blend spaces and comparison conditions; <code>trigger</code> arms a one-shot that a matching transition consumes; <code>play</code> bypasses conditions when you need a specific state right now. Prefer parameters and transitions — they keep timing in the authored graph instead of scattered through scripts.</p>

